WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:10.540
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I am going to turn now to
item nine on the agenda which is the report

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.580
on NHS England performance, which, Karen,
may I invite you to present?

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:20.640
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: Yes, I will just go through
this quickly. I will not repeat what is in

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:28.820
the report. The first section just gives an
overview of the ten corporate priorities and

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:36.180
the subset of programmes that sit within those
corporate priorities. We have been doing a

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:41.680
lot of work on our assurance associated with
those programmes. We are gaining increasing

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:48.500
confidence - I won't say it is complete confidence
- in our assessment. We are planning a series

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:56.049
of further stock takes during October and
November in order to ensure that we have got

00:00:56.049 --> 00:01:02.909
as much confidence as we need on delivery
by the end of the year against our business

00:01:02.909 --> 00:01:10.080
plan targets, and we will make sure that then
we will be taking any remedial action. Indeed,

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.550
we have some remedial action in place and
contingency work in place with some key programmes,

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:19.510
such as learning disabilities for example.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:25.690
I was not going to say anything more on the
risks section. I am happy to take any questions

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:32.880
there. I just wanted to touch a couple of
things on the corporate operations performance.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:39.730
A couple of things; one is that I am afraid
there is a small error in one of the numbers,

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:47.490
which is the report against the FOI numbers
which was reporting 96.2%. It is actually

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.110
84.9%.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:55.130
There was a system error which caused us to
have wrong data. We are still within the target

00:01:55.130 --> 00:02:01.720
so it is still green, but I just wanted you
to be aware of that. What the report is telling

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:09.530
us is that we are reaching a degree of stability
post our OACP, although there are some significant

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:19.630
changes that we are planning and still working
through on some workforce changes associated

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:27.890
with IQ, leadership academy and patient safety
changes, which we are working through. That

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:33.500
is the reason for the future workforce change
in the next quarter.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:42.400
You will note the large number of people who
transitioned out of NHS England on 1st September,

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:48.260
which was associated with PCS and the staff
who transitioned to Capita. I am happy to

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:55.680
tell you that, so far so good; that is working
well. Relationships are good. It is obviously

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:03.280
not great for staff, but they have landed
well in the organisation and I think things

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.730
are looking good for performance there at
present.

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:15.319
It is not in the report but we have had a
recent staff barometer, and that showed a

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:23.870
small improvement on the last staff barometer.
So we are up about 2% on virtually all of

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:30.099
the indicators. Absolutely no room for complacency,
we have got much more work to do on that,

00:03:30.099 --> 00:03:36.670
but it has been extremely helpful in helping
to inform another programme of work that we

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:44.480
have just launched on improving NHS England.
Some of the key areas that staff reported

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.599
on that we were already working on, we are
tackling through that programme and have work

00:03:49.599 --> 00:03:58.910
streams on some pretty basic stuff: accommodation,
recruitment, procurement, but also engagement

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:07.260
and diversity as key areas that we want to
improve on, and aspects of our controls environment

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:15.099
as well, so controls, assurance and governance.
All of that work is being initiated, and much

00:04:15.099 --> 00:04:20.289
of it is already under way. That was really
I wanted to say at this stage.

00:04:20.289 --> 00:04:25.870
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you Karen. May I invite
questions and comments from the Board.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:32.249
&gt;&gt;Ciaran Devane: Thanks Karen, and thank you
for the accurate reflection of the state of

00:04:32.249 --> 00:04:39.680
things. My worry would be that, if we have
ten priorities plus a couple of subsets, and

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:43.520
none of them are green and all of them are
amber and sometimes with a bit of red, you

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:45.740
could say that is a really stressed organisation.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:57.520
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: Yes you could, but I think
it reflects that partly we are trying to be

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:04.740
strongly objective. Some of those programmes
have been going for a while but many of them

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:10.589
started up at the start of the year and are
new programmes of work. Some of them - mental

00:05:10.589 --> 00:05:17.219
health, cancer, learning disabilities - will
all have big implications for implementing

00:05:17.219 --> 00:05:23.960
in the field, and we are putting in place
the delivery structures that will enable us

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:33.430
to do that work, but clearly these are complex
and high risk programmes. The ratings do reflect

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:44.779
a degree of both the risk, as well as our
ability to deliver plans on target. Do not

00:05:44.779 --> 00:05:52.110
read into it that amber means we are missing
all of our targets. What it does mean is that,

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:59.339
if you look at both the progress against target
and the degree of risk, amber is trying to

00:05:59.339 --> 00:06:07.659
reflect both of those. Many of those are in
a good place in terms of targets, but we wanted

00:06:07.659 --> 00:06:10.210
to reflect the degree of risk associated with
them as well.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:13.899
&gt;&gt;Ciaran Devane: Will people working on them
feel that they are in control and it is going

00:06:13.899 --> 00:06:17.189
in the right direction, or will they feel
stressed?

00:06:17.189 --> 00:06:24.409
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: What is pleasing, I think,
is that we have got oversight groups and governance

00:06:24.409 --> 00:06:31.139
groups associated with all of these priorities,
and they have been incredibly positive in

00:06:31.139 --> 00:06:36.909
bringing the whole of NHS England's teams
together to work on these priorities rather

00:06:36.909 --> 00:06:48.110
than working in silos, and obviously reflecting
the wider NHS participants where that is relevant

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:56.129
to the particular priority. Not all of those
teams have all of the capability, both leaderships

00:06:56.129 --> 00:07:02.199
skills and resource that they need. Some are
still building, and certainly that is true

00:07:02.199 --> 00:07:07.830
in some of the new care models where the teams
are still building up.

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:15.539
We are in the process of recruiting some new
SROs, National Implementation Directors, and

00:07:15.539 --> 00:07:21.610
we are trying to appoint very capable and
experienced programme directors in some of

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:29.490
the more challenging and difficult programmes
as well. I think we are making the right progress

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:35.699
in building that capability. Clearly, there
is a lot of implementation going on. These

00:07:35.699 --> 00:07:41.039
are complex areas. It is not just about NHS
England doing things, it is about impacting

00:07:41.039 --> 00:07:47.179
on the whole of the NHS, and the whole of
the NHS is in a stressed state as we have

00:07:47.179 --> 00:07:51.229
all talked about. Again, they are not without
risk.

00:07:51.229 --> 00:08:02.459
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Karen, a number of these
delivery programmes have been red for three

00:08:02.459 --> 00:08:12.289
months or more. Do we have the right help
and support mechanism for those programmes,

00:08:12.289 --> 00:08:20.849
and the right QA and other interventions that
will help those programmes to get to a different

00:08:20.849 --> 00:08:21.309
trajectory?

00:08:21.309 --> 00:08:26.949
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: We are definitely trying
to put that in place. You are particularly

00:08:26.949 --> 00:08:34.779
probably pointing out learning disabilities.
We are working very closely with Jane and

00:08:34.779 --> 00:08:40.740
the programme team. Jane, you may want to
contribute on this. In fact, we have a specific

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:49.890
deep dive with Simon and the team to think
about how we bring the right thinking to do

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:57.600
with contingencies, alternative plans and
alternative approaches in order to try to

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:05.830
ensure that we focus on what we need to deliver.
I think we are trying to do that. I will not

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:09.700
say it is perfect, I will not say we have
got everything we need in place, but we absolutely

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:11.620
are trying to do that.

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:20.820
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings I can add a bit to that. Some
time ago now we established some new NHS England

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:26.860
programme boards that I chair, each of which
has senior link representation at director

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:30.510
level or national director level from the
other directorates that have an influence

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:36.320
or need to contribute this programme. We have
had a couple of sessions with Karen. We have

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:42.640
a much more detailed review on Tuesday with
Simon, in terms of our proposals for the national

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:44.330
transformation plan.

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:51.250
Actually, if you look at what we said we would
do, we have delivered everything we said we

00:09:51.250 --> 00:10:01.910
would do on time, so far this financial year.
So far on delivery, we are amber, amber, green.

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:06.070
However, we know that there have been many
years that people have actually tried to make

00:10:06.070 --> 00:10:11.160
a difference to people with learning disability
and failed. We know it is complex. It does

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:18.490
involve working across the LGA, ADAS, Health
Education England, CQC, providers and clinicians,

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:20.880
and it is a very complex programme.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:26.570
I think the amber/red rating reflects the
complexity of the programme. It does not mean

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:31.410
that we are at all complacent or that we are
not actually taking action to continue looking

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:39.520
at what we can do to improve. As Karen has
referred to, we have just advertised for another

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:46.760
national implementation director/SRO that
will actually be able to concentrate full

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:52.590
time on really driving through the delivery
that we need to make once we have determined

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:56.590
what that national transformation plan and
bed closure plan will look like over the next

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:58.560
few years.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:06.750
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Karen, just one item that
you referred to was PCSS. This is something

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:11.560
that the Board has taken a close interest
in over far too long, simply because -

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:26.660
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Who knew you could make a
piece of paper ring, that is incredible. That

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:27.070
is personalised stationery.

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:31.630
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: No, I am deeply impressed
and hugely embarrassed. Anyway Karen, as I

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:37.750
was saying before I so rudely interrupted,
we were conscious that the PCSS transfer programme

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:42.150
was going to be immensely technically difficult,
and then for legal reasons it took longer

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:47.810
than it might have done. I think we would
just like to record our appreciation at the

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:55.360
safe transfer of this responsibility, and
the continued loyalty of our staff under very

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:59.010
difficult conditions. We are grateful for
that, and we are grateful to you and to the

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:02.450
programme board for bringing this to fruition
so thank you.

00:12:02.450 --> 00:12:04.940
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: Thank you very much, thank
you.

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:08.700
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Are there any other comments
on that paper? No. Thank you very much.

